~ SLIM Playground — Demo Video Script
@slim: 2.0
@title: SLIM Playground Demo Video Script
@version: 1.0
@author: Sasidhar Nagandla
@date: 2026-05-15
@+purpose: Step-by-step recording guide for the SLIM playground demo video


::OVERVIEW
Total runtime target: 4-5 minutes (full version), 2:25 (LinkedIn cut)
Audience: developers, AI engineers, prompt engineers
Goal: show the playground converting real files with visible token savings
Recording setup: Chrome or Edge, 1920x1080, browser zoom 110%, maximised window

Clip structure for LinkedIn short cut:
  Hook (0:15)     — homepage hero comparison, token count numbers
  Main (1:30)     — Scenario 1 full flow (agent config Markdown)
  Proof (0:30)    — token savings bar + real file upload
  CTA (0:10)      — "free, open source, link in comments"


::OPENING_HOOK
Duration: 0:00 – 0:25

Narration:
  Every token you send to an LLM costs money and burns context.
  Most Markdown files waste 30-50% of those tokens on formatting
  syntax the model doesn't need. SLIM strips that waste out —
  automatically.

On screen:
  Show the homepage hero comparison panel.
  Let the viewer read the Markdown side vs the SLIM side.
  Point to the token counts: ~118 tokens vs ~69 tokens.


::SCENARIO_1
Duration: 0:25 – 2:25
Label: Agent Config — Markdown to SLIM
Why first: visual, concrete savings, the most common real-world use case

Step 1 — Open playground
  Navigate to playground.html.
  Show the empty state: two panels, input left, output right.
  Point out the format buttons (Markdown / JSON / Plain Text).

Step 2 — Load the example
  Click the "Load example..." dropdown.
  Select "Agent config (Markdown)".
  Let the input panel fill.

Step 3 — Walk through the input (pause here ~15 sec)
  Point out each element and say what it costs:
    YAML frontmatter block (--- ... ---) — metadata the LLM doesn't need
    **bold** and *italic* markers throughout
    - bullet list markers on every item
    Fenced code block with triple backticks

Step 4 — Watch the conversion
  The output panel fills in real time.
  Narrate as each transformation becomes visible:
    "YAML front-matter became @headers — stripped before the LLM sees them"
    "Bold markers gone. Italic markers gone. Text meaning preserved."
    "Bullet markers stripped — indentation still shows the list structure"
    "Code block became ::CODE_1 python — verbatim, named, one :: token"

Step 5 — Token stats bar
  Point to the stats row at the bottom of the input panel.
  Read the numbers out loud: Input X tokens, LLM-facing Y tokens, Saved Z%.
  Let that number sit on screen for 2-3 seconds.

Step 6 — Copy the output
  Click the Copy button in the output panel header.
  Say: "Ready to paste directly into any LLM API call."


::SCENARIO_2
Duration: 2:25 – 3:25
Label: JSON Config
Why: shows SLIM is not Markdown-only

Step 1 — Switch format
  Click the JSON button in the input panel header.
  The placeholder text changes.

Step 2 — Load the example
  Click "Load example..." -> select "Config (JSON)".

Step 3 — Walk through the output
  Each top-level JSON key became a named ::SECTION json block.
  Narrate:
    "JSON gets restructured into named sections."
    "Each section is verbatim — no transformation, just clearly labelled."
    "The LLM sees structured, named data instead of one flat JSON blob."

Step 4 — Token savings
  Point to stats bar. JSON savings are typically 15-25%.
  Say: "Even JSON gets leaner."


::SCENARIO_3
Duration: 3:25 – 4:25
Label: Your Own File — Drag and Drop
Why: the wow moment — works on real files instantly

Preparation (before recording):
  Have a real Markdown README or agent config ready on the desktop.
  Ideal: 50-100 lines, lots of formatting, some code blocks.
  Good candidates: a project README, a Copilot agent .md file,
  any system prompt written in Markdown.

Step 1 — Drag and drop
  Drag the .md file from the desktop onto the playground window.
  Narrate: "Drag and drop any .md, .json, or .txt file directly."

Step 2 — Watch conversion
  Show the real file converting instantly.
  Don't narrate — let the token numbers speak.

Step 3 — Colour legend walkthrough
  Point to the colour legend at the bottom of the output panel.
  Walk through each colour quickly:
    @key       grey    stripped, orchestrator-only
    @+key      teal    LLM-visible header
    ~ comment  green   stripped from LLM
    ::SECTION  purple  named content section
    $var       orange  interpolated variable

Step 4 — Download
  Click the "Download .slm" button.
  Show the file saves with the .slm extension.
  Say: "Use it in your pipeline, commit it to your repo, send it to any LLM."


::SCENARIO_4_OPTIONAL
Duration: 4:25 – 4:55 (optional, include for a longer cut)
Label: Docs Quick Tour

Step 1 — Navigate to docs.html
  Show the sidebar: Schema, Sections, Variables, Directives.

Step 2 — Sections entry
  Scroll to the Sections heading.
  Show ::NAME type and :::NESTED type with the live example.
  Narrate: "Named sections are the core of SLIM v2."

Step 3 — Migration Guide
  Scroll to the Migration Guide section.
  Show the two-line diff:
    Before: === BLOCK [type] ... === /BLOCK
    After:  ::BLOCK type
  Say: "One line, no close tag, one fewer token per block boundary."


::CLOSING
Duration: 4:55 – 5:10 (or 4:25 – 4:35 on the short cut)

On screen:
  Return to the homepage.
  Let the stats bar sit: 43.3% average token savings.

Narration:
  "SLIM is open source, MIT licensed, no signup required.
  Paste your file, copy the output, start saving tokens today.
  Link in the comments."

Call to action text overlay (if editing):
  slimformat.org
  Free. Open source. MIT.


::RECORDING_TIPS
Browser setup:
  Use Chrome or Edge
  Resolution: 1920x1080
  Zoom: 110% (makes text readable without crowding)
  Hide bookmarks bar, extensions — clean browser chrome
  Maximise the playground window

Cursor:
  Use a cursor highlighter tool (e.g. Cursor Highlight on Windows)
  Viewers need to track where you are clicking

Audio:
  Record narration live alongside screen, or add in post-production
  Avoid long silences during conversion — talk through it
  Script the narration from this doc before recording

Captions:
  Add captions — most LinkedIn video is watched muted
  Keep caption lines short (under 8 words per line)

File prep:
  Load all example files before recording — no fumbling
  Have your real demo .md file on the desktop, named clearly
  Test the drag-and-drop before the take

Editing cuts to make:
  Cut any hesitation pauses longer than 1 second
  Speed-ramp the "watching it convert" moments to 1.5x
  Hold on token savings numbers for at least 2 seconds
  Add a zoom-in punch on the savings percentage


::NARRATION_SCRIPT
Full word-for-word script for recording or voice-over:

Opening (0:00):
  Every token you send to an LLM costs money and burns context.
  Most Markdown files waste 30 to 50 percent of those tokens on
  formatting that the model doesn't need. SLIM strips that waste
  out — automatically.

Scenario 1 intro (0:25):
  This is a typical agent config written in Markdown.
  Let's load it into the playground.

Scenario 1 conversion (1:00):
  Watch what happens in real time.
  The YAML front-matter became header lines — they'll be stripped
  before the LLM ever sees them.
  Bold markers, italic markers, gone. The meaning stays.
  List bullet markers stripped — indentation still shows the structure.
  And the code block became a named section: ::CODE_1 python.
  Verbatim content, clearly labelled, no closing tag needed.

Token stats (1:45):
  [read the numbers out loud from the stats bar]
  That's [X] percent fewer tokens sent to the model.
  On a large agent config or long system prompt, that compounds fast.

Scenario 2 intro (2:25):
  SLIM isn't just for Markdown. Let's try JSON.

Scenario 2 output (2:45):
  Each top-level key became a named section.
  The LLM sees structured, labelled data instead of one flat blob.
  Even JSON gets leaner.

Scenario 3 intro (3:25):
  Now let's try a real file. I'll drag and drop it directly.

Scenario 3 colour legend (3:50):
  Grey headers are stripped before the LLM.
  Teal headers are LLM-visible.
  Green lines are comments — gone.
  Purple marks named sections.
  Orange marks variable references.

Closing (4:55):
  SLIM is open source, MIT licensed, no signup.
  Paste your file, copy the output, start saving tokens today.
  Link in the comments.
